agents/ — generic executor subagents
=======================================
(File is extensionless on purpose: the agent loader scans *.md, and a
README.md without agent frontmatter risks a startup-breaking parse error.)

What lives here
---------------
One file per model, plus one file per role-specialized tier. The bulk of the
roster is GENERIC executors: the model is pinned, the role comes entirely
from the task prompt. Two single-model tiers carry their role in the file
instead — `verifier-*` (runs tests/linters/harnesses, reports results) and
`researcher-*` (fetches external sources, reports with URLs and quotes) —
because their permission shapes differ. Tiers stay single-model: execution
output is deterministic and research fan-out is serial, so neither benefits
from model diversity.

Naming: <model-slug>-<tier> for executors; <role>-<model-slug>-<tier> for the
specialized tiers. Cost tiers are COST, not capability:
  -free   no-cost models (6 executors; verifier and researcher also free)
Dots in model ids become hyphens; a redundant trailing -free is collapsed
(e.g. mimo-v2-6-flash-free, not mimo-v2-6-flash-free-free).

Paid tiers: Disabled for now. Free only.

Data terms
----------
Informational only — nothing run through these agents is confidential, so
data terms are not a selection criterion. Kept because they are cheap to
record here and would matter if that changes (source:
opencode.ai/docs/zen "Privacy", re-checked 2026-10-16):
- Zero-retention: deepseek-v4.1-flash-free, glm-5.3-flash-free,
  space-bunny-free, longcat-2.5-preview-free.
- Logged / used for improvement: mimo-* (2.6, 2.5), ll-alpha-free (30-day
  retention), big-pickle. nemotron-* runs on NVIDIA free endpoints: "Trial
  use only — do not submit personal or confidential data."
- Trains on prompts and completions: muse-spark-1.3-contributor-free — the
  recorded price of the contributor tier.
- cat-jam-free: no training, 30 days retention for abuse monitoring.
Terms change with the free period — re-check before adding or swapping.

Speed and reliability (observed)
--------------------------------
deepseek-v4.1-flash-free and muse-spark-1.3-contributor-free are the fast,
reliable defaults, with glm-5.3-flash-free joining them as the preferred
trio (reported fast; reliability unconfirmed — its first smoke-test spawn
aborted under provider load on 2026-10-16, so fall back to deepseek or muse
if it stalls). nemotron-3-ultra-free is also reliable (not frontier) —
useful when a third independent opinion is wanted. space-bunny-free and
mimo-v2.6-flash-free are capable but markedly slower and often fail or
rate-limit — use them deliberately, never as defaults. step-5-preview-free
was retired from the free tier on 2026-10-16.

Prompt: role frame + follow-the-task-prompt + one pointer line to
protocols/executor-protocol.md (shared behavior: BLOCKED, denial handling,
sandbox discipline). Deliberately minimal — output formats and specs belong
in per-task briefs, which is where the review-orchestration and adviser-chat
skills put them.

Permissions (three profiles — least privilege per role)
-------------------------------------------------------
Executors observe and report; only the primary acts. Base profile (all
files): read tools, protocols/ + /tmp/ dirs, git read-only
subcommands (plain and `git -C` forms), read-only text tools
(wc/sort/uniq/cut/tr/jq/rg/grep, space-required form so bare invocations
can't hang on stdin). Denied everywhere: edit (covers writes), subagent
(executors are terminal), execute (Code Mode — its `fetch` egress and
session/worktree utilities bypass the other rules), all other bash.
- Executors: base profile, webfetch/websearch denied. Hermetic by
  construction — output derives only from repo and prompt.
- Verifier: base profile plus execution-shaped bash (pytest, npm test,
  go/cargo test, tsc, ruff, eslint, node --check, node *.js*). No direct
  edit tools; execution can still write (test logs), so the promise is
  containment, not incorruptibility. Project-specific runners belong in
  per-project agent files, not here.
- Researcher: base profile plus webfetch/websearch. Treats fetched content
  as untrusted data; every external claim needs URL + verbatim quote.
Rationale: `sed`/`awk`/`find`/`xargs`/interpreters stay denied on executors:
their safe and destructive modes are indistinguishable by prefix. Verified
live: a chain (`;`, `&&`, `||`, `|`, `( )`) is checked per segment — every
command in it must individually match the allowlist, or the whole chain is
denied. (Chains of allowlisted commands still run; chaining composes within
the allowlist, not past it.) Each tier's boundary is restated subagent-side in ../protocols/
(executor-protocol.md, verifier-protocol.md, researcher-protocol.md). Rules 1–4 are identical across the three files —
keep them in sync when editing; the sync note lives here, not in the
protocol files, because those are read on every spawn.

Adding a model
--------------
1. Copy the nearest-tier file. Set `model:` to the exact provider/model id and
   rewrite the one-line description ([TIER] + blurb + generic-executor line).
   Caution: filenames hyphenate dots but `model:` keeps the registry's exact
   string (e.g. file `muse-spark-1-3-...` but `model: opencode/muse-spark-1.3-...`).
   Verify with `opencode models` — a near-miss fails only at spawn, not at load.
2. Keep the permission block and two-sentence prompt byte-identical.
3. Add the new agent ID to the Roster section in
   ../skills/review-orchestration/SKILL.md (full names only — short names do
   not resolve).
4. Run `scripts/verify-roster.sh` and fix anything it reports: it checks
   every `model:` value against `opencode models` and every executor agent
   against the Roster section of ../skills/review-orchestration/SKILL.md
   (verifier-*/researcher-* are intentionally absent — single-model tiers,
   not reviewers; a missing entry fails only at orchestration time, not at
   load).
5. Restart opencode (agents/skills/permissions load at startup).

Retiring a model: delete its file, remove it from the skill roster list,
restart. If in doubt, keep the file — a stale roster entry costs nothing
until invoked, while a missing one breaks saved prompts that name it.
